---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import { doc, guides, releases, repo } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['install', '1. Install'],
  ['sign-in', '2. Sign in'],
  ['first-task', '3. Run a first task'],
  ['extend', '4. Add extensions'],
  ['trust', 'Permissions and data'],
  ['help', 'Troubleshooting'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Install and start"
  description="From a fresh install to your first task. Start with one provider and add extensions and services later."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="install">
    <h2>1. Install pi and Phosphor</h2>
    <p>
      You need Node.js 22.19 or newer and a provider account or API key. Install pi in a terminal:
    </p>
    <pre
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Install pi"><code>npm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent</code></pre>
    <p>
      <a href={`${releases}/latest`}>Download Phosphor from the latest release</a>. On macOS, open
      the DMG and drag Phosphor to Applications. On Linux, use the AppImage or the .deb. On Windows,
      run the x64 .exe installer.
    </p>
    <p>Or, on macOS or Linux:</p>
    <pre
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="Install Phosphor on macOS or Linux"><code>curl -fsSL https://github.com/agustinsacco/Phosphor/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
    <p>
      The script checks release checksums. It’s still a script piped into your shell, so <a
        href={`${repo}/blob/main/tools/scripts/install.sh`}>read it first</a
      >. Windows builds aren’t signed, so SmartScreen may warn you. Once you’ve confirmed the
      download came from this repo, choose More info → Run anyway.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Check:</strong> launch Phosphor. If the setup screen says pi is missing, make sure <code
        >pi --version</code
      > works and that its directory is on the PATH desktop apps see. Restart Phosphor after changing
      PATH.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="sign-in">
    <h2>2. Sign in to a provider</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>ChatGPT Plus/Pro:</strong> Settings → Accounts → sign in to the ChatGPT/Codex provider.
        This uses pi’s built-in OAuth. You don’t need the Codex CLI.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claude Pro/Max:</strong> install the official Claude Code CLI and the pi provider package,
        then manage logins under Settings → Extensions → Claude Code. <a href="/guides/claude-code/"
          >Step-by-step guide</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GitHub Copilot or Kimi For Coding:</strong> use the corresponding sign-in in Settings
        → Accounts. Copilot Enterprise Server uses pi’s terminal login.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>API keys or custom endpoints:</strong> set them up in pi. See pi’s docs on <a
          href="https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/providers.md"
          >provider auth</a
        > and <a
          href="https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/models.md"
          >custom models</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If sign-in from Settings doesn’t finish, run <code>pi</code> in a terminal and use <code
        >/login</code
      >. Claude Code logins are separate from pi’s provider credentials. Phosphor doesn’t come with
      any model usage. Your provider’s billing and plan limits apply.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="first-task">
    <h2>3. Run a first task</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open a repo you trust. A throwaway project is a good place to start.</li>
      <li>
        Pick a model in the composer. To use your Claude subscription, pick a model under <code
          >pi-claude-cli</code
        >, not one of the API-billed Anthropic entries.
      </li>
      <li>
        Leave the new worktree option on to get a separate checkout, or turn it off to work in the
        main checkout. Git features need a Git repo.
      </li>
      <li>
        Ask for something small you can check, like: “Add a test for an empty input in this parser.
        Run the relevant tests and show me the diff.”
      </li>
      <li>
        Expand the tool calls to see the commands and their output. Open Changes to review each
        file. Don’t take “done” at face value: check that the tests actually ran and passed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Want a write-up you can keep? Ask for it as an artifact and it opens as a versioned doc next
        to the chat. Use Files or Terminal to look at or run things yourself.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>Check:</strong> you know which branch the session is on, you’ve read the diff, and you’ve
      seen the test output. For a second task, start a new lane with its own worktree. Don’t point two
      sessions at the same checkout.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('worktrees.md')}>Worktree safeguards</a> · <a href={doc('chat.md')}
        >Chat and composer reference</a
      > · <a href={doc('routines.md')}>Schedule recurring work with routines</a>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="extend">
    <h2>4. Add extensions</h2>
    <p>
      Once coding works, add what you need. pi packages add web search, MCP services, subagents, and
      computer use. Install only what you’ll use, then start a new session.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-grid">
      {
        guides.slice(1).map(({ href, title }) => (
          <a href={href}>
            <h3>{title} →</h3>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="trust">
    <h2>Permissions and data</h2>
    <p>
      Phosphor runs on your machine. Unless you point it at a local model, the model doesn’t: your
      provider receives every prompt and tool result sent to it. Connected services have their own
      permissions and data policies.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Tools run with full permissions by default.</strong> There’s no approval prompt by default
      and no sandbox around the agent. A worktree separates Git checkouts; it doesn’t limit file or network
      access. Packages run code inside pi’s process. Read a package’s source before installing it, and
      only connect services you’re comfortable handing to the agent.
    </p>
    <p>
      HTML artifacts the model writes are isolated from the app and have no network access. That
      protects you from the page, not from the agent’s tools. <a href={doc('extensions.md')}
        >How extensions and artifacts work</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="help">
    <h2>If setup stalls</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>pi missing:</strong> check the PATH desktop apps see, not just your terminal’s.
      </li><li>
        <strong>No models:</strong> check that you’re signed in, and start a new session after changing
        provider config.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Claude is unavailable:</strong> use the <a
          href="/guides/claude-code/#troubleshooting">Claude troubleshooting checklist</a
        >.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Connector won’t authenticate:</strong> check its scopes and run <a
          href="/guides/connectors/#verify">Test in MCP Connectors</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a href={doc('updates.md')}>How updates work for each install type</a> · <a
        href={`${repo}/issues`}>Report a problem</a
      >. Include versions and the error. Strip tokens, account IDs, private paths, and anything
      sensitive from logs and screenshots first.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
